//? Title: Simon: actual probability support and binary recovery
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A promised two-to-one black box hides a nonzero XOR period that must be
recovered from equal outputs. Quantum interference makes input measurements
orthogonal to that period. This classical statevector demonstration computes the
full marginal, recovers the period from its support, and retains the original
four-bit period1011.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

For the canonical promised function f(x)=min(x,x XOR s), each output pairs x and
x XOR s. After Hadamards on the input register their amplitudes add with a phase
factor1+(-1)^(y dot s), so forbidden parity cancels:

$$P(y)=\begin{cases}2^{-(n-1)},& y\cdot s=0\pmod2,\\0,&y\cdot s=1\pmod2.\end{cases}$$
\alttext(The input marginal is uniform on strings orthogonal to the period and zero on the other strings.)

Zero is a valid measurement with positive probability. The historical figure
incorrectly omitted it and substituted unit bars for probabilities. Here all2^n
marginal entries are actual computed probabilities, including zero. The legacy
observed_y field names enumerated nonzero support, not sampled observations.
Their binary row rank is n-1, leaving a one-dimensional nullspace whose nonzero
vector is s.

The quantum query advantage concerns actual oracle measurements and enough
independent rows, in expectation order n queries. This simulator enumerates full
support using two registers with4^n amplitudes and is exponentially expensive
classical computation. It neither samples observations nor demonstrates quantum
runtime on hardware. Low-order bit indices match native gate indexing.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/simon_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <numeric>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <array>
#include <tuple>
#include <bit>
#include <datamunge/random/quality.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/simon.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = a::simon_trace(2, 3);
    std::print("| y | actual probability |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t y = 0; y < r.probabilities.size(); ++y)
        std::print("| {} | {} |\n", y, r.probabilities[y]);
    std::print("\n| actual pivot bit | actual reduced rows |\n| ---: | --- |\n");
    for (const auto &s : r.reduction)
        std::print("| {} | `{}` |\n", s.column, s.rows);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Two bits, hidden XOR period 3");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("Actual support rows {}",
                                     r.result.observed_y));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Rank {}, recovered {}", r.rank,
                                     r.result.recovered_period));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/simon_tiny.svg");
}
```
